snapshot/.planning/phases/48-survivable-state-writes-and-honest-gate-recovery/48-REVIEWS.md:68:- **What goes wrong:** a crashed writer can leave an orphan with pid P and seq 0. A later process reusing pid P then fails its first write with `AlreadyExists`, until a sweep (`clear_state` or `Gates::cleanup`) removes the orphan. The failure is visible rather than a silent loss, and it needs both a crash and pid reuse.
snapshot/.planning/phases/48-survivable-state-writes-and-honest-gate-recovery/48-REVIEWS.md-69-- **Fix direction:** on `AlreadyExists`, retry with the next sequence number or add a random nonce. Test with a planted orphan carrying the same pid.
